At launch, Ladybird re-adopts the session rows of the windows the last
run left open, and reads a tab’s history only once the tab is selected.
Its PinnedOnly policy splits each window’s pinned tabs from the rest, as
Firefox’s #prepDataForDeferredRestore() does: Firefox restores the
pinned tabs (browser.sessionstore.restore_pinned_tabs_on_demand is
false) and puts the rest on its closed-window list. Chromium’s
PinnedTabCodec::WritePinnedTabs() instead flattens every normal window’s
pinned tabs into prefs::kPinnedTabs as plain URLs.

When the last window closes, Ladybird counts it as left open, as
Chromium’s SessionService::WindowClosing() does by parking the
last trackable window in pending_window_close_ids_ rather than
committing its close. Ladybird’s crash guard matches Firefox’s
#needsRestorePage(), which stops restoring automatically once recent
crashes exceed browser.sessionstore.max_resumed_crashes (1); it
leaves out Firefox’s six-hour session-age rule. And Chromium’s
StartupBrowserCreatorImpl::RestoreOrCreateBrowser() never restores
automatically after a crash at all.
